AI Moderation Documentation
1. What is AI Moderation?

AI Moderation is the process of automatically reviewing user-generated content (UGC) like posts, comments, images, or text to:

Detect offensive content (hate speech, harassment, explicit content, etc.)

Detect spam or unwanted behavior

Enforce platform guidelines automatically

Provide real-time feedback to users before submission

In your app, we will use OpenAI’s moderation endpoint (omni-moderation-latest) for text content.

2️. Why Use AI Moderation?

Prevent toxic content before it appears to other users

Reduce manual moderation efforts

Automatically enforce community standards

Immediate feedback: users see why their post/comment was blocked

3️. Supported Content Types

Text: posts, comments, descriptions, messages

Optional Future Expansion: images, videos (requires other AI models)

4️. Integration Architecture

Here’s a high-level flow:

User Input (Post/Comment)
          |
          v
   Validation Layer
  (Empty, Length, Format)
          |
          v
   AI Moderation Layer
(OpenAI Moderation API)
          |
          v
  Safe?   No -> Error Message to User
  Yes      |
          v
  Save Content in DB
          |
          v
Return Success Response

5️. Step-by-Step Implementation
Step 1: Setup OpenAI

Sign up for OpenAI and get API key

Install OpenAI SDK:

npm install openai

OpenAI model = "model: "omni-moderation-latest""

free alternatives - Google Perspective API


Create a service for moderation:

import OpenAI from 'openai';

const openai = new OpenAI({
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY,
});

Step 2: Create a Moderation Service

This is a central place to check if content is safe.

export class ModerationService {
  async moderate(content: string): Promise<{ flagged: boolean; categories: any }> {
    const response = await openai.moderations.create({
      model: 'omni-moderation-latest',
      input: content,
    });

    const result = response.results?.[0];
    return {
      flagged: result?.flagged || false,
      categories: result?.categories,
    };
  }
}


flagged → true if content violates rules

categories → detailed breakdown of why it was flagged

Step 3: Integrate With Posts

Before saving a post:

const result = await moderation.moderate(postDescription);

if (result.flagged) {
  throw new Error('Post contains inappropriate content and cannot be added.');
}


If safe → save post in DB.

Step 4: Integrate With Comments

Before saving a comment:

const result = await moderation.moderate(comment);

if (result.flagged) {
  throw new Error('Comment contains inappropriate content and cannot be added.');
}


If safe → save comment in DB.

Step 5: Combine With Validation

Before AI moderation, always validate input:

Post: minimum 5 characters

Comment: minimum 3 characters

Not empty

Example:

if (!comment || comment.trim().length < 3) {
  throw new Error('Comment must be at least 3 characters');
}


Order is important:

1️. Validate locally → length, empty
2️. AI moderation → offensive content
3️. Save to DB

Step 6: Handle Errors at Frontend

Send clear error messages from backend:

"Comment must be at least 3 characters"

"Comment contains inappropriate content"

Frontend should display the message in UI instead of generic "Failed to add comment"

Step 7: Optional Enhancements

Flag Instead of Block:

Instead of rejecting, mark content as flagged = true in DB

Show only to moderators

Custom Rules:

Combine AI moderation with custom word lists (slurs, banned words)

Real-time Feedback:

Check content while user types (optional, throttled requests to AI)

Analytics:

Track flagged posts/comments for reporting and moderation insights

6️. Benefits

Safe community → reduces toxic behavior

Automatic enforcement → fewer moderators required

Better UX → users understand why content was rejected

7. Summary

Validation first, then AI moderation, then DB save

Clear error messages sent to frontend

Can handle posts, comments, and any UGC

Extendable to flagging, reporting, analytics